From aef64c777928d54fa7aa8b42ba8e0274f0350b35 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vishal Kumar Swain <vkswain50@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 22:04:16 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] neurosym: extend local model evaluator, default to the
 C++ solver

Local model evaluator (neurosym_conv.h/.cpp): local_eval_bv/local_eval_bool/
local_eval_array_at recursively resolve bit-vector arithmetic, comparisons,
and array select/store chains directly from NeuroSym own model output,
reducing (not eliminating) how often get_bv()/l_get() need to fall back to
--neurosym-model-prog for a real counterexample trace. Anything not covered
(floating-point, uninterpreted functions, an array never constrained by a
reachable store) still falls through to the model-solver fallback exactly
as before this evaluator existed.

Default --neurosym-prog changed from "python main.py %f" to
"$HOME/bin/neurosym-cpp-solve %f": NeuroSym has been reimplemented in
C++ with its own native SMT-LIB2 parser (no Python involved at solve time),
so the bare --neurosym flag now runs that by default instead of the
original Python entry point. The Python solver remains fully available via
an explicit --neurosym-prog override.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
